@page "/getting-started"

<PageTitle>Getting started · Bcrdt</PageTitle>

<div class="bb-intro">
    <h1>Getting started</h1>
    <p class="bb-subtitle">
        One package for the engine and the Blazor components, one for the transport, one for the relay - then a
        <code>BcrdtTextArea</code> inside a <code>BcrdtCollaborationScope</code> is a working collaborative editor
        with live presence, automatic reconnection and offline support.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Install</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        <code>Bit.Bcrdt</code> carries the engine, the synchronization, presence and persistence, the in-memory and
        WebSocket transports and the Blazor components. The SignalR client transport is a package of its own with no
        ASP.NET Core server dependency, so it runs in WebAssembly and Hybrid apps too. All four packages target .NET 10
        and 11.
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code"><span class="c"># the engine and the Blazor components</span>
dotnet add package Bit.Bcrdt

<span class="c"># the SignalR transport - or use the WebSocket transport built into Bit.Bcrdt, or none</span>
dotnet add package Bit.Bcrdt.SignalR</pre>
    <div class="bb-scroll">
        <table class="bb-table">
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Package</th><th>Contents</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td class="bb-table-key">Bit.Bcrdt</td>
                    <td>The engine - <code>BcrdtText</code>, <code>BcrdtArray</code>, <code>BcrdtMap</code> and <code>BcrdtSet</code>, updates, snapshots and selective undo - with presence, the in-memory, file-system and IndexedDB stores, the sync protocol, <code>AddBitBcrdtServices</code>, the input bindings, the in-memory and WebSocket transports, metrics, and the Blazor components: <code>BcrdtCollaborationScope</code>, <code>BcrdtTextArea</code>, <code>BcrdtPresenceView</code> and <code>BcrdtComponentBase</code>.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="bb-table-key">Bit.Bcrdt.SignalR</td>
                    <td>The SignalR client transport.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="bb-table-key">Bit.Bcrdt.Server</td>
                    <td>The ASP.NET Core relays - <code>BcrdtHub</code> for the SignalR transport and <code>MapBitBcrdtWebSocket</code> for the WebSocket transport - with per-document read-only or read-write access, and <code>BcrdtDocumentHost</code>, which keeps a stored replica of every document the relays carry.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="bb-table-key">Bit.Bcrdt.Testing</td>
                    <td>A deterministic, seeded network simulator, random operation generators, a convergence harness and an update corrupter for fuzzing - for testing a collaborative feature, or a type of your own, against loss, duplication, reordering and partitions.</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Register the services</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        Register it once, in every project that renders collaborative components - in a Blazor Web App, that is both
        the server and the client project. The .NET SignalR client needs an <strong>absolute</strong> hub URL, so
        resolve it against the host; in Blazor WebAssembly that is the host page's base address.
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code"><span class="k">using</span> Bit.Bcrdt;
<span class="k">using</span> Bit.Bcrdt.SignalR;

<span class="k">string</span> hubUrl = <span class="k">new</span> Uri(<span class="k">new</span> Uri(builder.HostEnvironment.BaseAddress), <span class="s">"hub/bcrdt"</span>).ToString();

builder.Services.AddBitBcrdtServices(collaboration =&gt; collaboration
    .UseTransport(<span class="k">new</span> BcrdtSignalRTransportFactory(hubUrl))
    .UseInMemoryStore());</pre>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        <code>AddBitBcrdtSignalRTransport(hubUrl)</code> is the same registration as a one-liner. Each of the three
        seams - transport, store and options - is optional: an application that configures none of them still gets a
        working, purely local collaborative document, so a feature can be built and tested without a server and then
        connected to one without changing how it reads or writes state.
    </p>
    <div class="bb-scroll">
        <table class="bb-table">
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Seam</th><th>Choices</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td class="bb-table-key">Transport</td>
                    <td><code>BcrdtSignalRTransportFactory</code> (SignalR), <code>BcrdtWebSocketTransportFactory</code> (a raw WebSocket, built in), or any <code>IBcrdtTransportFactory</code> of your own. None: the document stays local.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="bb-table-key">Store</td>
                    <td><code>UseIndexedDbStore()</code> in the browser - a document, and what was typed into it offline, survives a reload or a closed tab; <code>UseStore(new BcrdtFileSystemDocumentStore("./documents"))</code> on a server, the desktop or MAUI; <code>UseInMemoryStore()</code> for tests; or any <code>IBcrdtDocumentStore</code>.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="bb-table-key">Options</td>
                    <td><code>Configure(options =&gt; ...)</code>: how long presence lasts (<code>PresenceTimeout</code>, 30 s), heartbeat and reconnection timing (<code>Sync</code>), compaction (<code>Persistence</code>), whether deleted content is reclaimed (<code>GarbageCollect</code>), and whether each document gets a default undo stack.</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <pre class="bb-code"><span class="c">// The WebSocket transport instead: {documentId} is filled in per document.</span>
builder.Services.AddBitBcrdtServices(collaboration =&gt; collaboration
    .UseTransport(<span class="k">new</span> BcrdtWebSocketTransportFactory(<span class="s">"https://example.com/ws/bcrdt/{documentId}"</span>))
    .UseIndexedDbStore());</pre>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> Map the relay</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        The hub lives in a separate server-side package, so that the client transport carries no ASP.NET Core server
        dependency. Use <code>AddBitBcrdtHub</code> rather than a bare <code>AddSignalR</code>: SignalR's default
        32 KB receive limit is smaller than the handshake reply for any document of real size.
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code">dotnet add package Bit.Bcrdt.Server    <span class="c"># in the server project</span></pre>
    <pre class="bb-code"><span class="k">using</span> Bit.Bcrdt.Server;

builder.Services.AddBitBcrdtHub();   <span class="c">// AddSignalR, with a receive limit sized for sync messages</span>
<span class="c">// ...</span>
app.MapHub&lt;BcrdtHub&gt;(<span class="s">"/hub/bcrdt"</span>);</pre>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        The raw WebSocket transport has its relay in the same package: call <code>app.UseWebSockets()</code> and map
        it with <code>app.MapBitBcrdtWebSocket("/ws/bcrdt/{documentId}")</code>. On its own a relay only relays: a
        document exists while somebody has it open. To keep every document on the server as well - so the first
        person into an empty room still gets everything ever written into it - register a document host, as the
        <a href="live">live page</a>'s server does:
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code">builder.Services.AddBitBcrdtDocumentHost(_ =&gt; <span class="k">new</span> BcrdtDocumentHostOptions
{
    Store = <span class="k">new</span> BcrdtFileSystemDocumentStore(Path.Combine(builder.Environment.ContentRootPath, <span class="s">"documents"</span>)),
});</pre>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        Who may join a document, and whether they may write, is decided by overriding
        <code>BcrdtHub.AuthorizeAccessAsync</code> (or setting <code>AuthorizeAccess</code> on the WebSocket endpoint).
        The <a href="live">live page</a> shows a read-only link enforced that way.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> A first editor</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        <code>BcrdtCollaborationScope</code> opens the document, renders its content with the document as context,
        shows <code>LoadingContent</code> while it opens (and <code>ErrorContent</code> if it cannot), follows
        <code>DocumentId</code> when it changes and releases the document when it goes away.
        <code>BcrdtTextArea</code> turns every keystroke into the minimal edit and publishes the caret through
        presence; <code>BcrdtPresenceView</code> lists who is here. There is no stylesheet to reference and no script
        tag to add: the one small module the textarea needs is imported on demand.
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code"><span class="k">@@using</span> Bit.Bcrdt
<span class="k">@@using</span> Bit.Bcrdt.Blazor

&lt;<span class="k">BcrdtCollaborationScope</span> DocumentId=<span class="s">"notes-42"</span> User=<span class="s">"_me"</span> Context=<span class="s">"document"</span>&gt;
    &lt;<span class="k">BcrdtPresenceView</span> Presence=<span class="s">"document.Presence"</span> /&gt;
    &lt;<span class="k">BcrdtTextArea</span> Text=<span class="s">"@@document.Text("body")"</span> Presence=<span class="s">"document.Presence"</span> /&gt;
&lt;/<span class="k">BcrdtCollaborationScope</span>&gt;

<span class="k">@@code</span> {
    <span class="k">private readonly</span> BcrdtCollaboratorInfo _me = <span class="k">new</span>(<span class="s">"u-1"</span>, <span class="s">"Ada"</span>, BcrdtPresenceService.SuggestColor(<span class="s">"u-1"</span>));
}</pre>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        <code>SuggestColor</code> hashes a user id into a fixed palette, so every participant agrees on everyone's
        colour without asking anyone. In a page that is prerendered and then becomes interactive, a document may only
        be opened once the component is interactive (<code>RendererInfo.IsInteractive</code>) - one opened while
        prerendering belongs to a request that is already over. <code>BcrdtCollaborationScope</code> waits for that by
        itself. The <a href="live">live page</a> is exactly this, across browser tabs.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> A component of your own</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        Derive from <code>BcrdtComponentBase</code> - the base of the three components - to make any component
        collaborative. Its <code>Track</code> overloads re-render the component whenever a type, a document, presence
        or the connection changes, from anyone; they marshal the notification onto the renderer, coalesce bursts onto
        a frame (<code>RenderInterval</code>, 16 ms) so five people typing do not cause hundreds of renders a second,
        and unsubscribe when the component goes away.
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code"><span class="k">@@inherits</span> BcrdtComponentBase

&lt;p&gt;@@_title.Count characters&lt;/p&gt;

<span class="k">@@code</span> {
    [CascadingParameter] <span class="k">public</span> BcrdtCollaborativeDocument Document { <span class="k">get</span>; <span class="k">set</span>; } = <span class="k">default</span>!;

    <span class="k">private</span> BcrdtText _title = <span class="k">default</span>!;

    <span class="k">protected override void</span> OnParametersSet()
    {
        _title = Document.Text(<span class="s">"title"</span>);
        Track(_title);   <span class="c">// re-render, coalesced, whenever the text changes - from anyone</span>
    }
}</pre>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> Working with a document in code</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        Outside a component, <code>IBcrdtCollaborationService</code> is the whole API. <code>OpenAsync</code> loads the
        document from the store first - so it is usable at once, and offline - then connects and lets the handshake
        reconcile whatever changed meanwhile. Opening the same identifier twice returns the same instance, reference
        counted, so pair every <code>OpenAsync</code> with a <code>CloseAsync</code>.
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code">BcrdtCollaborativeDocument document = <span class="k">await</span> collaboration.OpenAsync(<span class="s">"board-7"</span>,
    <span class="k">new</span> BcrdtOpenDocumentOptions { User = me });

<span class="k">using</span> (<span class="k">var</span> transaction = document.BeginTransaction())   <span class="c">// one update, one round of events</span>
{
    document.Map.Set(<span class="s">"title"</span>, <span class="s">"Q3 planning"</span>);
    document.Array(<span class="s">"attendees"</span>).Push(<span class="s">"Ada"</span>, <span class="s">"Grace"</span>);
    document.Text(<span class="s">"notes"</span>).Insert(0, <span class="s">"Agenda:\n"</span>);
}

document.Changed += change =&gt; { <span class="c">/* every committed update, with its origin */</span> };
document.Text(<span class="s">"notes"</span>).Observe((change, transaction) =&gt; { <span class="c">/* change.Delta */</span> });

<span class="k">await</span> collaboration.CloseAsync(<span class="s">"board-7"</span>);</pre>
    <ul class="bb-detail-list">
        <li><strong>Connection.</strong> <code>ConnectAsync</code> and <code>DisconnectAsync</code> take a document
            online and offline on purpose; <code>ConnectionStatus</code>, <code>IsSynced</code> and
            <code>Sync.LastSyncedAt</code> say where it stands. A dropped connection is retried by itself.</li>
        <li><strong>Values.</strong> A document holds <code>null</code>, <code>bool</code>, <code>string</code>,
            integers, <code>double</code>, <code>float</code>, <code>decimal</code>, <code>byte[]</code>, dates,
            <code>Guid</code>, lists and string-keyed dictionaries of those, and nested collaborative types. Anything
            else is refused up front; an object of your own is flattened onto those with <code>SetObject</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Threads.</strong> A document is not thread-safe. One opened through the service applies every
            network update on the synchronization context it was opened on - in Blazor, the renderer's.</li>
        <li><strong>The engine alone.</strong> <code>document.Crdt</code> is the replica itself, a
            <code>BcrdtDocument</code> that runs anywhere with no service at all - a console app, a worker, a test.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        The <a href="internals">internals page</a> shows transactions, updates and change events as they happen.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-danger"></span> Choose the data type</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        This is the decision that matters most: the merge is decided by the data structure rather than by your
        application, so it is worth knowing what each one will decide.
    </p>
    <div class="bb-scroll">
        <table class="bb-table">
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Type</th><th>Concurrent edits resolve as</th><th>Reach for it when</th><th>See it</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key">BcrdtText</td><td>Both edits survive, each author's run stays contiguous</td><td>Anything a person types into at length</td><td><a href="text">Collaborative text</a>, <a href="rich-text">Rich text</a></td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key">BcrdtArray</td><td>Both insertions survive, in a stable order</td><td>Lists, ordered collections</td><td><a href="list">Collaborative list</a></td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key">BcrdtMap</td><td><strong>One value wins</strong>, deterministically but arbitrarily</td><td>Fields where a later value legitimately replaces an earlier one</td><td><a href="form">Collaborative form</a></td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key">BcrdtSet</td><td>An add wins over a concurrent remove - if it added something (re-adding a present element is a no-op)</td><td>Tags, membership, flags</td><td><a href="form">Collaborative form</a></td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        The trap is a <code>BcrdtMap</code> with a long string in it: two people editing that field concurrently
        means one of them loses everything they typed. If a field is something a human types prose into, make it a
        nested <code>BcrdtText</code> and both edits merge - the <a href="form">collaborative form</a> shows the two
        side by side.
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code">document.Map.EnsureText(<span class="s">"description"</span>).Insert(0, <span class="s">"..."</span>);   <span class="c">// merges</span>
document.Map.Set(<span class="s">"description"</span>, <span class="s">"..."</span>);                    <span class="c">// one side wins</span></pre>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        The same caution applies to <code>BcrdtArray.Replace</code> and <code>Move</code>: both delete the element and
        insert a new one, so an edit made to the element at the same time is lost, and two people moving one element
        at once end up with two copies. Change a nested type in place when that matters. The
        <a href="conflicts">conflict lab</a> runs each rule on purpose.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Every hosting model</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        The registration is the same everywhere, and <code>IBcrdtCollaborationService</code> is registered
        <strong>scoped</strong> on purpose. In WebAssembly and Hybrid apps - MAUI, WPF, Windows Forms - there is one
        scope, so each app is one replica. On Blazor Server each circuit is a scope, so every connected user gets a
        replica and a client identity of their own; a singleton would make every visitor one replica. In a Blazor Web
        App, register it in both projects: whichever side renders interactively owns the replica.
    </p>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        On Blazor Server a circuit outlives its browser tab for a while, and keeps publishing presence until it goes. To
        withdraw a user the moment the connection drops, call <code>builder.Services.AddBitBcrdtCircuitPresence()</code>
        from <code>Bit.Bcrdt.Server</code>: its <code>CircuitHandler</code> calls <code>document.Presence.Suspend()</code>
        on every open document when the connection goes down, and <code>Resume()</code> when it comes back. The engine
        needs no JavaScript at all, so it runs as well in a background service or a console app. Outside AOT builds, a
        WebAssembly app can also <a href="getting-started#lazy-loading">lazy load</a> the library instead of registering it.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card" id="lazy-loading">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> Lazy loading on WebAssembly</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        A WebAssembly app can fetch Bcrdt only when someone opens a part of the app that collaborates. It then cannot
        call <code>AddBitBcrdtServices</code> in the <code>Program.cs</code> of the project that runs in the browser,
        and configures the collaboration service instead: <code>IServiceProvider.GetBcrdtCollaborationService(...)</code>
        takes the builder <code>AddBitBcrdtServices</code> takes, and where no <code>IBcrdtCollaborationService</code>
        is registered, <code>BcrdtCollaborationScope</code> - and the <code>Collaboration</code> property of every
        <code>BcrdtComponentBase</code> - use the service it configured. That is WebAssembly only: everywhere else Bcrdt
        is loaded with the app, so the registration is required there, and the components fail without it. Mark the
        assembly as lazy loaded in the project that runs in the browser, and load it before the first page that uses it
        renders:
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code"><span class="c">&lt;!-- The browser project's .csproj --&gt;</span>
&lt;<span class="k">ItemGroup</span>&gt;
  &lt;<span class="k">BlazorWebAssemblyLazyLoad</span> Include=<span class="s">"Bit.Bcrdt.wasm"</span> /&gt;
&lt;/<span class="k">ItemGroup</span>&gt;</pre>
    <pre class="bb-code"><span class="c">@@* App.razor - in a Blazor Web App, the client project's Routes.razor: the router waits for OnNavigateAsync
   before it renders the page. *@@</span>
<span class="k">@@using</span> Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.WebAssembly.Services
<span class="k">@@inject</span> LazyAssemblyLoader LazyAssemblyLoader

&lt;<span class="k">Router</span> AppAssembly=<span class="s">"typeof(App).Assembly"</span> OnNavigateAsync=<span class="s">"OnNavigateAsync"</span>&gt;
    ...
&lt;/<span class="k">Router</span>&gt;

<span class="k">@@code</span> {
    <span class="k">private async</span> Task OnNavigateAsync(NavigationContext context)
    {
        <span class="c">// The pages that collaborate: fetch Bcrdt before they render. A second call loads nothing again.</span>
        <span class="k">if</span> (context.Path.StartsWith(<span class="s">"notes"</span>, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase))
            <span class="k">await</span> LazyAssemblyLoader.LoadAssembliesAsync([<span class="s">"Bit.Bcrdt.wasm"</span>]);
    }
}</pre>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        Those pages, or a layout only they use, configure the service in <code>OnInitialized</code> - which runs before
        the components inside them ask for it - with what <code>Program.cs</code> would otherwise have registered:
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code"><span class="c">@@* NotesLayout.razor *@@</span>
<span class="k">@@using</span> Bit.Bcrdt
<span class="k">@@inherits</span> LayoutComponentBase
<span class="k">@@inject</span> IServiceProvider Services
<span class="k">@@inject</span> NavigationManager Navigation

@@Body

<span class="k">@@code</span> {
    <span class="k">protected override void</span> OnInitialized() =&gt; Services.GetBcrdtCollaborationService(collaboration =&gt; collaboration
        .UseTransport(<span class="k">new</span> BcrdtWebSocketTransportFactory(Navigation.BaseUri + <span class="s">"ws/bcrdt/{documentId}"</span>))
        .UseIndexedDbStore());
}</pre>
    <ul class="bb-detail-list">
        <li>Leave <code>AddBitBcrdtServices</code> out of that project's <code>Program.cs</code>: calling it references Bcrdt's types before the assembly is there. For the same reason only what renders after the assembly has loaded may use Bcrdt's types - the pages it is loaded for and a layout only they use, not <code>App.razor</code>, <code>Routes.razor</code>, a layout every page shares or <code>Program.cs</code>.</li>
        <li>No type in an assembly the router reads when the app starts - the app's own assembly first of all - may derive from or implement one of Bcrdt's: no component that inherits <code>BcrdtComponentBase</code>, no transport, store or client id provider of your own. On WebAssembly such a type fails to load before Bit.Bcrdt is there, and keeps failing after it has loaded. Put it in a library that is lazy loaded together with Bit.Bcrdt.</li>
        <li>Configure the service before anything asks for it: in <code>OnInitialized</code>, not after an <code>await</code> in <code>OnInitializedAsync</code>, which lets the layout's content render first. A component that asks before anything configured the service throws, naming both ways to provide one; nothing is created by that, so a configuration that comes later - on the next page, say - still takes effect.</li>
        <li>Only the first configuration creates the service, so a page visited again gets the same service back. <code>GetBcrdtCollaborationService(_ =&gt; { })</code> configures a purely local one, with no transport and no store.</li>
        <li>The builder's container holds the app's <code>IJSRuntime</code>, so <code>UseIndexedDbStore()</code> works, and nothing else of the application's: hand the transport and the store over as instances or delegates.</li>
        <li>With the SignalR transport, lazy load <code>Bit.Bcrdt.SignalR.wasm</code> along with <code>Bit.Bcrdt.wasm</code> - and the SignalR client's own assemblies too, where nothing the app loads at startup uses them.</li>
        <li>Do not lazy load in an AOT build. AOT compiles every assembly into <code>dotnet.native.wasm</code>, so there is nothing left to download later, and lazy loading an AOT-compiled assembly can fail at startup.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        In a Blazor Web App only the browser lazy loads; the server project loads Bcrdt with the rest of the app:
    </p>
    <ul class="bb-detail-list">
        <li>The router has to run in the browser for <code>OnNavigateAsync</code> to load the assembly there: give <code>Routes</code> the <code>InteractiveWebAssembly</code> or <code>InteractiveAuto</code> render mode in <code>App.razor</code>. With per-page render modes the router and the layouts render statically on the server, so <code>OnNavigateAsync</code> never runs in the browser - and neither does a layout's <code>OnInitialized</code>.</li>
        <li>The server project still calls <code>AddBitBcrdtServices</code>. Prerendering - and, with <code>InteractiveAuto</code>, the visits that run in a circuit - use that registration: <code>GetBcrdtCollaborationService(...)</code> returns it there without calling the configuration, so the same layout works on both sides.</li>
        <li>The server project also registers <code>LazyAssemblyLoader</code>, which <code>Routes.razor</code> injects while it is prerendered there, and which loads nothing outside the browser: <code>builder.Services.AddScoped&lt;LazyAssemblyLoader&gt;()</code>.</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Where to go next</h3>
    <ul class="bb-detail-list">
        <li><a href="text">Collaborative text</a> - three replicas of one paragraph, presence and carets, and what happens when one goes offline or loses its connection.</li>
